\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.0.1}{\ignorespaces The running tree \(T\) with the ancestor-query overlay. Below each node: depth \(d\) (root at \(0\)) and parent \(p\); above each node: \(\tin  /\tout  \) from the entry-only DFS of Chapter~5 (Chapter~\ref {ch:c24}), children visited in ascending label order, \(\tout  \) = the last stamp inside the subtree. The orange dashed spine \(1\!-\!2\!-\!5\!-\!8\) is one root-chain, so \(\ancestors  (8)=\{8,5,2,1\}\). Sanity checks used across the chapter: node~10 is the unique depth-4 node; \(u\preceq v\) iff \(\tin  (u)\le \tin  (v)\le \tout  (u)\) --- e.g.\ \(7\preceq 10\) as \(8\le 10\le 10\), while \(9\preceq 6\) fails as \(9>7\). Every tour, table and climb in 16.2--16.5 re-uses exactly these stamps.}}{958}{figure.caption.31}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c11-tree}{{16.0.1}{958}{The running tree \(T\) with the ancestor-query overlay. Below each node: depth \(d\) (root at \(0\)) and parent \(p\); above each node: \(\tin /\tout \) from the entry-only DFS of Chapter~5 (\chref {c24}), children visited in ascending label order, \(\tout \) = the last stamp inside the subtree. The orange dashed spine \(1\!-\!2\!-\!5\!-\!8\) is one root-chain, so \(\ancestors (8)=\{8,5,2,1\}\). Sanity checks used across the chapter: node~10 is the unique depth-4 node; \(u\preceq v\) iff \(\tin (u)\le \tin (v)\le \tout (u)\) --- e.g.\ \(7\preceq 10\) as \(8\le 10\le 10\), while \(9\preceq 6\) fails as \(9>7\). Every tour, table and climb in 16.2--16.5 re-uses exactly these stamps}{figure.caption.31}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16.0.2}{\ignorespaces The chapter's five-state taxonomy: rows 1--3 are the three machines for the question "\(\lca  (u,v)=?\)", row~4 is the same lifting machine carrying a payload (the merged old-Chapter-12 topic), row~5 is what the hinge \emph  {measures}. Every solved problem in the chapter sits on exactly one row: CSES~1135 on row~2, CSES~1687 on row~3, CF~1304E on row~4, CF~208E on row~5, the Summit CF~1062E across rows 2 and 5.}}{959}{figure.caption.32}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:c11-taxonomy}{{16.0.2}{959}{The chapter's five-state taxonomy: rows 1--3 are the three machines for the question "\(\lca (u,v)=?\)", row~4 is the same lifting machine carrying a payload (the merged old-Chapter-12 topic), row~5 is what the hinge \emph {measures}. Every solved problem in the chapter sits on exactly one row: CSES~1135 on row~2, CSES~1687 on row~3, CF~1304E on row~4, CF~208E on row~5, the Summit CF~1062E across rows 2 and 5}{figure.caption.32}{}}
